+</helmet>
+<div class="doc" style="width: 1200px; box-sizing: border-box; padding: 48px; background: #f9f6f1; color: #251e18; font: 400 15px/1.45 system-ui, sans-serif">
+  <h1 style="margin: 0 0 8px; font: 600 32px/1.2 Georgia, serif">Decisions</h1>
+  <p style="margin: 0 0 28px; color: #59514a">One argument per open question in 04 §3. Each names the choice, then what lost and why.</p>
+  <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 28px 40px">
+    <section><h2>§3.1 The page’s structure</h2>
+      <p><b>One page in question order, whose top half rearranges by state.</b> The sticky strip, then the answer, then What needs you, The line, What is coming, Window, How it went. On a phone it is one column, and What needs you shows two items, so the line stays one scroll away. At 1440 it is two columns.</p>
+      <p>The reshape is structural. With nothing to do, the answer is a plain line, What needs you does not exist, The line takes the wide column, and the log rises. With six things wrong, a filled band appears and the wide column belongs to the person.</p>
+      <p class="lost">Lost: a page per question (the morning glance would become four taps, and the urge to interrupt needs one surface); a live half and a history half (nowhere for the first question); different structures per device (two pages to learn, and both are primary).</p></section>
+    <section><h2>§3.2 One vocabulary over several sets</h2>
+      <p><b>The eight roles of 02, as words, each with one key cap.</b> Eight is learnable in a morning because five are ordinary English and two are already the outcome verbs. Rows print their recorded word, a class always by its own name, and the mark beside it carries the role. The legend is built from the mapping in code, so it cannot drift.</p>
+      <p>It does not need to be visible at all times: the mark is always next to a recorded word, so a reader never decodes a glyph alone. The strip links to it on every viewport.</p>
+      <p class="lost">Lost: a smaller set of four (it would fold stale into finished well and unknown into waiting, which claim more than the records do); showing all sets raw (six vocabularies at a glance).</p></section>
+    <section><h2>§3.3 Needs a person reads first</h2>
+      <p><b>The question and the recommendation in place; the whole decision one tap away; the answer on the pull request.</b> Those two lines let a person decide whether to go now. Options and costs stay in the fold because the answer is written elsewhere anyway. A stop with only a handoff shows its reason and links to the handoff; a stalled pickup or double claim shows its key.</p>
+      <p>Order: <b>To decide</b> (parks and stops settled on a pull request), then <b>To check</b>, oldest first in each. Grouping by what you do beats pure age, which would mix a two-minute answer with an investigation. Repository is shown, not sorted on. The only filled band and filled mark on the page belong to this role.</p>
+      <p class="lost">Lost: a badge colour on the same layout (the brief forbids it); showing full frames in place (one park would fill a phone).</p></section>
+    <section><h2>§3.4 Waiting, hung and parked</h2>
+      <p><b>No word “hung”. Each fact keeps its recorded word and its own evidence.</b> <span style="font-family: monospace">waiting 1 h 25 min, wall reset passed 16:10, nudge sent 16:15</span>; <span style="font-family: monospace">drifting, threads flat 3 rounds: 6 6 6 6</span>; <span style="font-family: monospace">launch record unknown, 5 h 37 min, holds its slot</span>; a takeover is a stage of its own, and working. A park holds no slot, so it never appears in a slot. A sentence on the row beats a shared “stuck” pill because the evidence is what tells them apart.</p>
+      <p>The kind of a wait has one row in Evidence, <span style="font-family: monospace">kind of wait</span>, which today reads “not reported by the runtime” or “usage limit”. A later runtime fills that value; nothing else changes.</p>
+      <p class="lost">Lost: a parked lane among the sessions (it suggests a slot is held); a heartbeat threshold (the records keep their own expectations).</p></section>
+    <section><h2>§3.5 Accounts, slots and walls</h2>
+      <p><b>Accounts and sessions are one surface. Each account is a row and each held slot is a box inside it.</b> Which session holds which slot is then a position, not a join. Pips and <span style="font-family: monospace">1 of 2</span> show fullness at a glance; <span style="font-family: monospace">at cap</span> and <span style="font-family: monospace">behind a wall, lifts 18:27, in 1 h 40 min</span> say why an account takes nothing. An unknown reset says so, with its backoff. On a phone boxes stack; a free account is one line.</p>
+      <p class="lost">Lost: a separate accounts panel (two lists that must be read together, and no phone width for both); a list of sessions with an account column (fullness has to be counted).</p></section>
+    <section><h2>§3.6 The queue</h2>
+      <p><b>Ordered by what will happen, not by source.</b> Starts when a tick finds a free slot (re-armable, <span style="font-family: monospace">drive</span> label), with the next free account named; held behind a leader, longest wait first; reported, not started; will not start. The source is a chip on every row.</p>
+      <p>Reported pickups get a heading of their own and one sentence: waiting for a tick will not start these. That is the misleading case, so it is stated once where it matters and kept visible in the compact view. An assigner who cannot write is also in What needs you.</p>
+      <p>A hand-added entry is a dashed cap at the queue’s foot with “not available”, always present, no field, no button, so it cannot look like a control.</p>
+      <p class="lost">Lost: one flat list by age (it would imply reported pickups are next); a disabled input (it looks like a capability that is merely off).</p></section>
+    <section><h2>§3.7 History and its filters</h2>
+      <p><b>Class counts are the class filter.</b> Presets and class chips are one tap and sit in the Window panel, above the fold; repository, stage and account are native selects; reason and range are typed. Every active filter is a chip with its own ×, plus Clear all. The state is one query string, shown in plain text, so a phone’s view is a link a laptop opens.</p>
+      <p>A row gives the time, class, target, account, length and the reason, on its own line, at a glance, because it is the one field that explains anything. Expanding gives the whole reason, the record and the link. Chips count ignoring the class filter, so choosing a class does not erase the others (a server change; see Handoff).</p>
+      <p class="lost">Lost: a filter drawer (hides state on the very screen where it matters); a table with sortable headers (the log has one useful order).</p></section>
+    <section><h2>§3.8 Links out</h2>
+      <p><b>Only what opens; the rest is text that says why.</b> A pull request is a real link, derived from repository and number on a GitHub-shaped forge, labelled by the act: “Answer on the pull request”. An item links when the tracker gives a URL; without one the row says “No tracker link” and names the key to look up, never an empty link. A receipt becomes a link once the server serves it.</p>
+      <p><b>What cannot open, or belongs elsewhere, copies.</b> One word, Copy, beside the thing: a workspace path, a record id, a key without a link, a command, a pull-request link, the link to this view. It suits this page because the phone sees what the laptop acts on, and the answer is written on the pull request. Copy answer line gives <span style="font-family: monospace">answer PRRT_x1: </span> with the option left blank, so the choice stays a person’s.</p>
+      <p>The page is served over plain http on a private address, where the browser’s clipboard API does not exist. Copy therefore falls back to a selected field, which worked on plain http in Chromium; if it fails the button says so, and the text stays selectable.</p>
+      <p class="lost">Lost: selectable text alone (poor on a phone); an editor scheme link (none exists and it fails silently on a phone).</p></section>
+    <section><h2>§3.9 Recurring classes</h2>
+      <p><b>Show recurrence; no chart.</b> A short block in the log lists each bad class on a repository that occurred on three or more of the last seven nights, with seven squares and its count, computed by the page’s query. The label is a button that applies that filter. One line says a person records it with <span style="font-family: monospace">reflect</span>; nothing else happens. A question about nights is a filter, so it stays one.</p>
+      <p>Time-series charts lost: the log is small (36 rows on the reference day), rates swing on small counts, and a trend line invites a reading the records do not support. Seven squares are text in CSS and need no library.</p></section>
+    <section><h2>§3.10 Staleness and the timer</h2>
+      <p><b>The strip states one fact on every viewport: “Tick 16:45, 2 min ago, next due 16:50.”</b> Each panel carries “as of the 16:45 tick”; the word “live” appears nowhere. Past the interval plus the longest tick the strip darkens and a banner says how long, and the answer turns past tense: “Nothing needed a person at the 16:00 tick.” It reads every 60 s and on focus, shown in the footer.</p>
+      <p>Server gone: the page keeps what it last had, says when, turns filters off and names the command. Wrong token: nothing was read, so nothing is shown, with what to do. Both state the fact and neither apologizes.</p>
+      <p class="lost">Lost: a pulsing “live” dot (it would imply a stream); a countdown (precision the timer does not have).</p></section>
+    <section><h2>§3.11 Shapes that recur</h2>
+      <p><b>Six earn promotion; see Handoff for each.</b> Status mark, Target line, Time and length, Account mark, Class with reason, Copy button. Each is on three or more surfaces.</p>
+      <p class="lost">Lost as primitives: Slot box (one surface), Queue row (one surface), Filter chip (a chip with a pressed state, not a new thing), Panel (already canonical).</p></section>
+    <section><h2>§3.12 Dependency-free in production</h2>
+      <p><b>Nothing here needs a library.</b> Disclosures are native <span style="font-family: monospace">details</span>, including term popovers, so fold and keyboard behaviour cost no script. Filters are native selects and inputs. Layout is grid, flex and container queries; themes are <span style="font-family: monospace">light-dark()</span>; marks are text and CSS; the person glyph is two gradients; the logo is inline SVG. The only script a page needs: fetch JSON, render rows, write the query string, copy text. This prototype runs in a canvas runtime for its Tweaks; none of its markup depends on one, and its logic is plain functions.</p>
+    </section>
+  </div>
+</div>
